HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ppaammttooppnngg was new in Netpbm 10.70 (June 2015).

       Before ppaammttooppnngg, the two ways to create PNG images with Netpbm were ppnn‐‐
       mmttooppnngg and ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg.  The history of the former is discussed above.
       The latter was added to Netpbm in 2005 as a cheap way to fill a signif‐
       icant need that ppnnmmttooppnngg did not: the ability to turn the alpha channel
       in a PAM image into the alpha channel in a PNG image.

       Handling  of the alpha channel with ppnnmmttooppnngg is very cumbersome (as was
       dealing with alpha channels in general before the introduction  of  the
       PAM  format).   ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg could do what people wanted with the alpha
       channel, but nothing else.  It was a very small program with  literally
       no command line options.

       The  goal in those days was eventually to expand ppnnmmttooppnngg to do the PAM
       alpha channel thing, rename it to ppaammttooppnngg,  and  retire  ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg.
       But  ppnnmmttooppnngg  is such a complex program, because of its dizzying array
       of features and its need for backward compatibility, that  adding  that
       one  capability  to it was a daunting task and for ten years nobody at‐
       tempted it.

       In 2015, one of the authors of the original ppnnmmttooppnngg  (from  before  it
       was  even  part of Netpbm -- a program that shared essentially no lines
       of code with ppnnmmttooppnngg of 2015) decided to go in a different  direction.
       While  many  features of ppnnmmttooppnngg were pretty important and easy to im‐
       plement, many others were probably of no use in the modern world or  at
       least  not  important enough to justify the complexity they lent to the
       code.  (The features thought to be outdated were  ones  that  were  in‐
       tended to make the PNG output slightly smaller - something considerably
       less important with the declining cost of computer resources).

       And there was an opportunity to drop those features: We could  use  the
       new  name 'pamtopng' for a new program, keep the existing program under
       the name 'pnmtopng', and avoid most backward compatibility trouble.

       Therefore, Willem van Schaik wrote an intermediate level  program  that
       had all the most important features of ppnnmmttooppnngg, plus the alpha channel
       handling of ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg, with nice, simple code.  That was ppaammttooppnngg.

       Because ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg had no options, ppaammttooppnngg was  backward  compatible
       with  it  without even trying.  Therefore, as soon as we added ppaammttooppnngg
       to Netpbm, we removed ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg and recommended that ppaammrrggbbaattooppnngg be
       installed as an alias for ppaammttooppnngg.



